# How names resolve

When a program writes `lower("ABC")`, the checker must find the one
declaration `lower` refers to. It may be the program's own function, a
function of an imported module, or a prelude function. The rules are fixed,
so a name always resolves to the same declaration, whatever order things are
written in.

Every module is an **import unit**: one scope in which its imports apply and
its names resolve. A program is an import unit of its own, the **program
module**. An import makes a module visible in the unit that imports it, and
nowhere else.

```d2
direction: down

name: "a name in a unit\nlower(\"ABC\")" {shape: page}
own: "declared by this unit?" {shape: diamond}
imported: "how many imports declare it?" {shape: diamond}

unit: "the unit's own declaration" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
one: "the imported declaration" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
none: "check error\nunknown-name" {style.stroke: "#b23b3b"}
many: "check error\nambiguous: qualify it,\nstring.lower(...)" {style.stroke: "#b23b3b"}

name -> own
own -> unit: "yes"
own -> imported: "no"
imported -> one: "one"
imported -> none: "none" {style.stroke: "#b23b3b"; style.stroke-dash: 5}
imported -> many: "two or more" {style.stroke: "#b23b3b"; style.stroke-dash: 5}
```

## Imports are per unit

A module's imports are its own. A module that imports `math` sees `math`, and
a program that imports the module does not see `math` through it. The
prelude, too, is the implicit import list of each unit separately.

## A unit's own names win

A declaration the unit writes itself wins over an imported declaration of
the same name, in every spelling: an unqualified call, a pipeline step and a
receiver call. The imported one stays reachable through its module. This
program declares its own `lower`:

```hql
fn lower(s: String) : String = "mine"
[lower("ABC"), "ABC" | lower, string.lower("ABC")]
```

```text
["mine", "mine", "abc"]
```

The program's `lower` takes no `self`, so the receiver spelling reaches
nothing, and the prelude's `lower` is not reached in its place:

```hql
fn lower(s: String) : String = "mine"
"ABC".lower()
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `lower` is this unit's own function, which takes no `self`, so `value.lower(…)` does not reach it and no import's `lower` over String is reached in its place; write `lower(value, …)`, or qualify the import's
 --> <expression>:2:1
  |
2 | "ABC".lower()
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

Because the unit's own declaration always wins, adding a function to an
imported module never changes what an existing program calls. The order in
which declarations are written does not matter either.

## Two imports, one name

When two imported modules declare the same name and the unit declares none,
the name is **ambiguous**, and a call that uses it is a check error. Write
the module name to choose one: `titles.initials(s)` or
`string.split(s, ",")`. The order of the imports never decides.

## A module is a closure

A name in a module resolves through the module's own declarations and its
own imports, and nothing else. A module never sees the program that imports
it. The bindings `hql` provides to a program, such as `vault`, `cards`,
`stdin` and `stdout`, are not bound in a module. What a module needs from a
program must be passed in, as a parameter:

````hql
module club.shelf

fn books(cards: Seq<Card>) : Seq<Card> = cards | filter(c => c.metadata.category == "book")
````

A program calls it with its own cards, `shelf.books(cards)`. A module written
this way is checked once, and behaves the same in every program that imports
it.

## Imports form no cycle

Imports cannot form a cycle. An import that would close one is an error, and
the error names the modules in the cycle. Two modules that need
each other are fixed by moving the shared part into a third module that both
import.

Module names form a tree, but the tree is not a dependency graph: `club` and
`club.reading` see nothing of each other unless one imports the other. A
child may import its parent. A parent that imports its child gets a warning.
